# Findings in git history that gitleaks is right to flag and that are not
# actionable, one line each, with the reason.
#
# Only a fingerprint that has been looked at belongs here. Blanket-allowlisting
# a path is what would make this gate worthless: testdata/fixtures/ is recorded
# from the live API, so it is exactly where a real credential would hide, and it
# is not excluded as a directory.
#
# Every entry appears twice because each change landed once on its branch and
# once as the squashed commit on main.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. A real access token, and the only entry here that was ever a credential.
#
# TestAWrappedTransportErrorCannotLeakACredential was written after a live token
# leaked into an integration-test log through a wrapped *url.Error. The test used
# the exact string that leaked, on the reasoning that a regression test should
# reproduce what failed — which committed a live credential to the repository, in
# a change whose subject was not leaking credentials.
#
# The token was a short-lived session token issued to the sandbox test account
# and committed on 2026-07-29; it grants nothing now, and the account it belonged
# to is a test account holding no real data. The working tree uses "deadbeef"
# repeated instead, which is the same shape and obviously nobody's.
#
# History is deliberately not rewritten: every commit hash
# would change, force-push would break the commit links in the merged pull
# requests that document how this project reached its decisions, and the token
# is long dead — the cost is real and the benefit is not.
